text:
Thou hast, good Lord! in many lands been heard;

And at my prayers to thee thy servants pray :

I know thou hearest all that men have said ;

Therefore I come before thine awful eye.

In the evening we would go to the shore
And walk along, my sister and I;

The tide was out, but it came in so near,
That once or twice we had to run for shelter on the sand;
But even then there were great heaps of seaweed,
Which had been swept up by the waves that night.

The man is dead, and his wife has died;
He used to talk about himself as being "old";
But what did he know? He had no wife!

I know not what I mean when I write these words,
For they are but mere lines upon this sheet of paper ;

But surely some one will find their meaning out;
They speak with no tongue that is now heard on earth —

The sun is out, and all day long it shines ;

 The summer's there; no need for any things ;

 No thought of rain or snow can ever come;

 But oh! how cold and bitter seems the tomb.

The stars are out, but not too near;

 The trees stand still and tall :

 In silence they behold him there ;

 But one whose heart is full with fear,
